{t:"Red Teaming", d:"Deliberately attacking your own AI system to find failures before users or adversaries do.", l:"intermediate", g:["safety","evaluation"],
 b:["Red teaming probes for harmful outputs, jailbreaks, prompt injection paths, data leakage, bias and tool misuse. It combines manual creative attack with automated adversarial generation, and it must cover the whole system — retrieval, tools, guardrails — not only the model.",
    "Findings should become permanent regression tests. A jailbreak found once and fixed without a test will be reintroduced by the next prompt change."],
 k:["Attack the full system: model, retrieval, tools and guardrails.",
    "Mix expert manual probing with automated adversarial generation.",
    "Every finding becomes a permanent test case.",
    "Re-run after every model or prompt change."],
 r:["Jailbreak","Prompt Injection","Guardrails","LLM Evaluation","Alignment"]},

{t:"Open-Weight Model", d:"A model whose trained parameters are published, so anyone can run, inspect, fine-tune and deploy it.", l:"intermediate", g:["ecosystem","llm"],
 b:["Llama, Mistral, Qwen, DeepSeek and Gemma publish weights under varying licences. That enables on-premises deployment for data-sensitive work, unrestricted fine-tuning, and freedom from a vendor's pricing and deprecation schedule.",
    "Open weights is not the same as open source: training data and code are usually withheld, and licences often restrict commercial use above a scale threshold. Self-hosting also transfers real operational cost — GPUs, serving infrastructure, monitoring and upgrades become your problem."],
 k:["Weights are published; training data and code usually are not.",
    "Enables on-premises deployment where data cannot leave the building.",
    "Licences vary considerably — read them before committing.",
    "Self-hosting trades API cost for operational burden."],
 r:["Foundation Model","Fine-Tuning","LoRA","Quantisation"]},

{t:"Latency", a:"TTFT and TPS", d:"How quickly a model responds — measured as time to first token and tokens per second thereafter.", l:"core", g:["operations","llm"],
 b:["Time to first token reflects the prefill phase: reading the prompt, which is compute-bound and scales with prompt length. Tokens per second reflects decoding, which is memory-bandwidth-bound and roughly constant per model.",
    "For interactive interfaces TTFT dominates perceived speed, which is why streaming matters so much — showing tokens as they arrive makes a ten-second response feel responsive. Shortening prompts and using prompt caching are the main levers on TTFT."],
 k:["TTFT is prefill (compute-bound); TPS is decode (bandwidth-bound).",
    "Streaming transforms perceived latency without changing total time.",
    "Shorter prompts and prompt caching are the main TTFT levers.",
    "Track p95 and p99, not the mean — tail latency is what users notice."],
 dg:"llm-inference",
 r:["Inference Cost","Prompt Caching","Speculative Decoding","Streaming"]},

{t:"Streaming", d:"Sending generated tokens to the client as they are produced instead of waiting for the complete response.", l:"core", g:["operations","llm"],
 b:["Streaming does not make generation faster; it makes waiting visible and tolerable. A response that takes eight seconds to complete but starts appearing after 400 milliseconds feels immediate. Server-sent events is the usual transport for HTTP APIs.",
    "It complicates the surrounding system: output guardrails cannot inspect a complete response before the user sees the start of it, structured output cannot be parsed until it is complete, and errors can occur mid-stream after a successful status code."],
 k:["Improves perceived latency dramatically; total time is unchanged.",
    "Server-sent events is the standard transport.",
    "Output filtering must run on partial text, or after the fact.",
    "Handle mid-stream errors — the HTTP status arrived long before the failure."],
 r:["Latency","Inference","Server-Sent Events","Guardrails"]},

{t:"Knowledge Cutoff", d:"The date after which a model has no training data, and therefore no reliable knowledge.", l:"core", g:["limitation","llm"],
 b:["A model's weights are frozen at training time. Anything after the cutoff — new library versions, recent events, changed APIs, current prices — is simply absent, and asking about it produces plausible invention rather than a refusal, unless the model has been trained to acknowledge the limit.",
    "Retrieval, web search tools and explicit date context in the prompt are the remedies. Models are also often unreliable about their own cutoff date, so do not ask the model — check the documentation."],
 k:["Knowledge is frozen at the training cutoff.",
    "Post-cutoff questions produce confident invention, not refusal.",
    "Fix with retrieval, search tools, or supplying current data in the prompt.",
    "Models frequently misreport their own cutoff — trust the docs instead."],
 r:["Hallucination","Retrieval-Augmented Generation","Large Language Model","Grounding"]},

{t:"Context Engineering", d:"Deliberately managing what occupies a model's context window — what to include, compress, summarise or drop.", l:"intermediate", g:["technique","llm"],
 b:["As agents and long conversations grew, prompt engineering expanded into a broader discipline: deciding which of the available history, retrieved documents, tool results and instructions actually belong in this request. Context is a scarce, expensive resource that degrades in quality as it fills.",
    "The standard techniques are summarising older turns, retrieving only what is relevant to the current step, trimming verbose tool output, placing critical information at the beginning or end where attention is strongest, and keeping static content first so prompt caching can work."],
 k:["Context is finite, expensive, and degrades in usefulness as it fills.",
    "Summarise old turns; retrieve rather than include everything.",
    "Put critical instructions at the start or end, not buried in the middle.",
    "Static prefix first, variable content last, to enable caching."],
 r:["Context Window","Prompt Engineering","Retrieval-Augmented Generation","Prompt Caching"]},

{t:"GPT", a:"Generative Pre-trained Transformer", d:"The decoder-only transformer family trained on next-token prediction that established the modern LLM pattern.", l:"core", g:["model","llm"],
 b:["GPT models use only the decoder half of the transformer, with causal masking so each position attends only to earlier ones. Train that on enough text and you get a general-purpose text generator; add instruction tuning and RLHF and you get an assistant.",
    "The lineage — GPT-2's surprising fluency, GPT-3's in-context learning, InstructGPT's alignment, ChatGPT's interface — is essentially the story of how the field arrived where it is. The architecture has become the default for generative language models."],
 k:["Decoder-only with causal masking — each token sees only the past.",
    "Trained purely on next-token prediction.",
    "In-context learning emerged at GPT-3 scale.",
    "The architectural template most generative LLMs now follow."],
 dg:"transformer",
 r:["Transformer","Large Language Model","BERT","Pre-training"]},

{t:"BERT", a:"Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers", d:"An encoder-only transformer trained by masking words, producing contextual embeddings for understanding tasks.", l:"intermediate", g:["model","nlp"],
 b:["BERT masks 15% of tokens and trains the model to predict them using context from both directions. That bidirectionality gives richer representations for understanding than a left-to-right model, which is why BERT dominated classification, named-entity recognition and question answering from 2018.",
    "It cannot generate text, since it has no causal ordering. Its descendants — RoBERTa, DeBERTa, and sentence-transformer variants — remain the practical choice for embeddings, classification and reranking, where they are far cheaper than an LLM."],
 k:["Masked language modelling gives bidirectional context.",
    "Encoder-only: excellent at understanding, incapable of generation.",
    "Still the efficient choice for classification, NER and embeddings.",
    "Cross-encoder BERT variants are the standard rerankers."],
 r:["GPT","Transformer","Embedding","Reranking","Named Entity Recognition"]}

